  "preamble": "Fronting of the postalveolar/palatal affricate /tʃ/ to the dental-alveolar affricate /ts/ keeps the affricate manner and voicing and only moves the place of articulation forward. It occurs independently in several South Asian lineages: Kashmiri and other Dardic languages and Nepali (where the inherited palatal series is pronounced [ts tsʰ dz dzʱ]). In Europe the same change is the Chakavian 'cakavism' of Istria and several Adriatic islands, where č is replaced by c. Polish mazurzenie is filed under the retroflex cell ʈʂ → ts (cz/sz/ż/dż [t͡ʂ ʂ ʐ d͡ʐ] > c/s/z/dz), not here.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fronting, Depalatalization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Kashmiri",
      "languageFamily": "Dardic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit catvā́raḥ 'four'",
          "to": "ċōr [tsoːr]",
          "note": "Turner's ċ marks the Kashmiri dental affricate /ts/ that regularly continues Old Indo-Aryan c (CDIAL 4655)."
        },
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit cōrá 'thief'",
          "to": "ċūr [tsuːr]",
          "note": "Same development word-initially (CDIAL 4931)."
        }
      ]
    },
